WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.170
We present InterPlay, a training-free framework for physically grounded video generation.

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:12.170
From a single image and a text prompt, a physics simulator and a video model share the dynamics of the scene.

3
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:18.670
The simulator moves the objects it can model. The video model generates everything else.

4
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:28.170
Image-to-video models produce convincing videos, but their physical interactions often violate basic physics.

5
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.170
Objects float, rest on a slope without rolling,

6
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:40.170
or the scene simply fails at the action that the prompt asks for.

7
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:50.170
Grounding generation in a physics simulator is a promising remedy. But existing pipelines use the video model as a mere renderer.

8
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.170
They cannot show what the simulator does not model, such as hands, animals or reflections.

9
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:05.170
They are also tailored to specific scenarios, need privileged inputs such as forces or materials, and often fail outright.

10
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:16.670
InterPlay splits the work. The simulator controls the objects it models reliably, and a frozen video model completes the rest.

11
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.170
A vision-language agent reads the image and the prompt, and writes a scene plan:

12
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:29.170
which objects to simulate, their materials, and the forces that trigger the event.

13
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.170
The planned objects are reconstructed in 3D:

14
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:41.748
segmented, lifted to 3D, and placed at metric scale. The agent inspects the result and refines it until it matches the photo and rests

15
00:01:41.828 --> 00:01:42.170
stably.

16
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.170
The simulator then rolls out the dynamics,

17
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:53.170
producing a control video of the simulated objects. The agent checks the motion and can revise its plan.

18
00:01:53.750 --> 00:02:00.279
Our two-phase conditioning ties the video model to the simulation. It relies on the two denoising transformers of the MiniMax H3 video

19
00:02:00.359 --> 00:02:05.409
model. The first steps use the reference network to follow the simulated motion. In the remaining steps,

20
00:02:05.489 --> 00:02:10.045
the anchoring network is used to capture the style and pin the first frame to the input image.

21
00:02:10.625 --> 00:02:17.545
This keeps the generation free to add what was never simulated: here, the hand that pulls the book.

22
00:02:20.625 --> 00:02:25.545
On the same inputs, InterPlay produces the physically expected outcome.

23
00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:31.045
The simulated objects stay physically correct,

24
00:02:31.625 --> 00:02:36.545
while the video model adds everything the simulator cannot:

25
00:02:37.125 --> 00:02:42.045
animals and hands,

26
00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:47.045
dust,

27
00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:52.545
reflections,

28
00:02:53.125 --> 00:02:57.545
and splashes.

29
00:02:58.125 --> 00:03:07.545
It handles balance, chain reactions, light, particles and wind, with no training, and from just an image and a prompt.
